title: Monitoring during androgen deprivation therapy
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Baseline:'' Complete [[counseling|Counseling before androgen deprivation therapy]]; document blood pressure, weight, cardiovascular history, falls/fractures, mood, and sexual function. Obtain PSA, testosterone, CBC, creatinine, liver tests/alkaline phosphatase, HbA1c/glucose, lipids, calcium, vitamin D, and ECG.
* ''Every 3–6 months:'' Review adherence, symptoms, blood pressure/weight, fatigue, mood/cognition, sexual function, and falls. Follow PSA/testosterone, hemoglobin, renal/liver function, and metabolic parameters. Combination agents require earlier, drug-specific checks; do not substitute this schedule for their monitoring protocols.
* ''Response:'' Confirm testosterone suppression around 12 weeks and with rising PSA; <50 ng/dL defines castration, although <20 is commonly achieved. If inadequately suppressed, check adherence/administration and reassess treatment before labeling resistance. Metastatic disease needs regular imaging even with stable PSA; new symptoms prompt reassessment.
* ''Bones:'' Assess FRAX (Fracture Risk Assessment Tool; ADT counts as secondary osteoporosis) and baseline DXA. NCCN permits omitting DXA in selected low-risk patients; EAU recommends it for long-term ADT. Reassess FRAX annually; repeat DXA every 1–2 years according to risk.
* ''Intervene:'' Under US osteoporosis criteria, offer [[Antiresorptive therapy]] for hip/vertebral fragility fracture, T-score ≤−2.5, or osteopenia with 10-year FRAX hip risk ≥3% or major-fracture risk ≥20%. Assess teeth, calcium/vitamin D, and renal function first; plan denosumab discontinuation to prevent rebound vertebral fractures. Osteoporosis regimens differ from bone-metastasis regimens; ADT alone does not justify metastatic-dose therapy.

Sources: NCCN Prostate Cancer v7.2026, PROS-G 6, PROS-B 1–3; ASCO SEP 2026, ch. 14, p. 768 (PDF 775); [[EAU 2026, §7.4|https://uroweb.org/guidelines/prostate-cancer/chapter/followup]].
